Since you're building your **AI Engineering projects in Python** and uploading them to GitHub, you should maintain a clean repository containing only the important source code, README, and required project configuration.

I'll give you a reusable `.gitignore` suitable for AI Engineering, Python, LLMs, LangChain, RAG, machine learning, and data science projects.

It will ignore:

- Virtual environments (`.venv`, `venv`, `env`).
- Python cache and compiled files.
- IDE settings and editor files.
- `.env` files and API keys.
- Jupyter Notebook checkpoints.
- Model weights and downloaded datasets.
- Logs, temporary files, and build folders.
- OS-generated files.

## **1. Recommended&#x20;**`.gitignore`**&#x20;file**

Create a file named exactly `.gitignore` in your project root.

````

# ==========================================
# PYTHON
# ==========================================
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.pyd
*.so
*.egg
*.egg-info/
.eggs/

# ==========================================
# VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENTS
# ==========================================
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/
virtualenv/
.python_env/

# ==========================================
# ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES / SECRETS
# ==========================================
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.template
secrets/
*.pem
*.key

# ==========================================
# IDE AND EDITORS
# ==========================================
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*.swn
.spyderproject/
.spyproject/
.cursor/
.history/

# ==========================================
# PYTHON TESTING AND TYPE CHECKING
# ==========================================
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.tox/
.nox/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage.xml/

# ==========================================
# JUPYTER NOTEBOOKS
# ==========================================
.ipynb_checkpoints/
*/.ipynb_checkpoints/

# ==========================================
# BUILD AND DISTRIBUTION
# ==========================================
build/
dist/
site/
pip-wheel-metadata/
wheels/
*.whl

# ==========================================
# LOGS AND TEMPORARY FILES
# ==========================================
*.log
logs/
tmp/
temp/
*.tmp
*.bak

# ==========================================
# AI / MACHINE LEARNING MODEL FILES
# ==========================================
*.pt
*.pth
*.ckpt
*.safetensors
*.onnx
*.h5
*.hdf5
*.pb
*.tflite
*.pkl
*.joblib

# ==========================================
# DATASETS AND GENERATED DATA
# ==========================================
/data/
/datasets/
/artifacts/
/outputs/
/checkpoints/
*.parquet

# ==========================================
# AI EXPERIMENT TRACKING
# ==========================================
wandb/
mlruns/
lightning_logs/
.tensorboard/
runs/

# ==========================================
# HUGGING FACE CACHE
# ==========================================
.cache/
huggingface_cache/
transformers_cache/

# ==========================================
# NODE / FRONTEND (IF USED)
# ==========================================
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
.next/
out/

# ==========================================
# OPERATING SYSTEM FILES
# ==========================================
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/

# ==========================================
# OTHER
# ==========================================
*.orig
*.rej
```
 
## 2. Files you should NOT ignore

Some beginners accidentally ignore important project files. Keep these in GitHub:

| File | Why keep it? |
|---|---|
| `README.md` | Project documentation |
| `requirements.txt` | Python dependencies |
| `pyproject.toml` | Project configuration and dependencies |
| `uv.lock` | Reproducible dependency versions |
| `poetry.lock` | Reproducible dependency versions |
| `.python-version` | Specifies Python version |
| `.env.example` | Shows required environment variables without secrets |
| `.gitignore` | Defines ignored files |

**Important:** Don't ignore all `.toml` files or Python version files. They are useful for reproducing your AI projects on another machine.

## 3. Recommended AI Engineering folder structure

For your future projects, I recommend this structure:

<box border radius="lg" padding={3} gap={2}>
  <row align="center" gap={2}>
    <icon name="folder" color="#D6A34A" size="lg"/>
    **AI-Engineering/**
  </row>
  <box padding={{left:4}} gap={2}>
    {#each [{icon:"file-text",name:"README.md",desc:"Project explanation"},{icon:"file-code",name:".gitignore",desc:"Ignored files"},{icon:"file-code",name:"requirements.txt",desc:"Dependencies"},{icon:"file-code",name:"pyproject.toml",desc:"Project configuration"},{icon:"folder",name:"week1/",desc:"Learning modules"},{icon:"folder",name:"week2/",desc:"Learning modules"},{icon:"folder",name:"projects/",desc:"AI projects"}] as item}
      <row align="center" gap={2}>
        <icon name={item.icon} color={item.icon==="folder"?"#D6A34A":"#6B9BD2"}/>
        <box flex="1" gap={0}>
          **{item.name}**
          <caption>{item.desc}</caption>
        </box>
      </row>
    {/each}
  </box>
</box>

Inside each week, you can maintain individual Python scripts and README files.

## 4. Apply `.gitignore` to your existing GitHub repository

Run these commands in PowerShell from your project root:

```powershell
# Check ignored files
git status

# Add updated gitignore
git add .gitignore

# Add remaining project files
git add .

# Commit
git commit -m "Add Python gitignore and clean project structure"

# Push
git push
```

If `.env` or other secret files were already committed, `.gitignore` alone won't remove them from Git tracking. Remove them from tracking using:

```powershell
git rm --cached .env
```

Then commit and push. Revoke any API keys that were exposed.

## 5. Verify which files are ignored

Use:

```powershell
git status --ignored
```

To check whether a particular file is ignored:

```powershell
git check-ignore -v .venv/
git check-ignore -v .env
```

---

**My recommendation:** Keep one reusable `.gitignore` in your main `AI-Engineering` repository. You can reuse it for all your future Python AI projects.

One additional tip: Don't upload large AI models or datasets directly to GitHub. Use Git LFS or external dataset/model storage when you actually need to version them.
````